WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.376 --> 00:00:01.209
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:01.209 --> 00:00:04.116
Welcome to today's Homeroom livestream.

00:00:04.116 --> 00:00:05.356
Today, it's just going to be me.

00:00:05.356 --> 00:00:08.016
So we're gonna do another ask me anything.

00:00:08.016 --> 00:00:09.626
So if you have any questions for me

00:00:09.626 --> 00:00:11.396
literally about anything,

00:00:11.396 --> 00:00:13.426
start putting them on the message boards

00:00:13.426 --> 00:00:15.396
on Facebook or YouTube,
wherever you're watching this.

00:00:15.396 --> 00:00:16.246
And we have team members

00:00:16.246 --> 00:00:18.756
who will surface the questions to me.

00:00:18.756 --> 00:00:20.726
So please start thinking about those.

00:00:20.726 --> 00:00:23.056
But before we jump into
the ask me anything,

00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:24.446
I'll give my standard announcement

00:00:24.446 --> 00:00:27.416
reminder that Khan Academy is
a not-for-profit organization

00:00:27.416 --> 00:00:28.866
and we can only exist through donations

00:00:28.866 --> 00:00:29.896
from folks like yourself.

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:31.036
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:31.036 --> 00:00:34.876
please think about going
to KhanAcademy.org/donate

00:00:34.876 --> 00:00:37.006
and making a donation.

00:00:37.006 --> 00:00:38.816
And our last guest where we talked

00:00:38.816 --> 00:00:40.496
about what makes folks happy,

00:00:40.496 --> 00:00:42.186
what are the things that
can actually make you

00:00:42.186 --> 00:00:44.556
a happier person is giving to others.

00:00:44.556 --> 00:00:46.526
So when you donate to Khan Academy,

00:00:46.526 --> 00:00:49.196
that is going to give to
many, many, many other folks

00:00:50.114 --> 00:00:52.296
the power of their potential
of their education.

00:00:52.296 --> 00:00:56.606
It's going to make you a much
happier person because of it.

00:00:56.606 --> 00:00:58.656
Also I'd like to give a shout out

00:00:58.656 --> 00:01:01.146
to several corporations who I'm guessing

00:01:01.146 --> 00:01:03.376
are also now happier corporations

00:01:03.376 --> 00:01:08.186
because they've stepped up as Khan Academy

00:01:08.186 --> 00:01:10.016
had to step up during COVID,

00:01:10.016 --> 00:01:11.426
our costs went up, our
server costs went up,

00:01:11.426 --> 00:01:13.386
we've been trying to accelerate programs

00:01:13.386 --> 00:01:14.826
and they helped us fill this gap.

00:01:14.826 --> 00:01:16.256
But we still need more help,

00:01:16.256 --> 00:01:19.276
but special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:01:19.276 --> 00:01:21.606
AT&amp;T, Fastly and Novartis.

00:01:21.606 --> 00:01:23.146
Also wanna remind folks

00:01:23.146 --> 00:01:27.466
about Homeroom with Sal, the podcast.

00:01:27.466 --> 00:01:30.706
The podcast version of
what we're doing right now

00:01:30.706 --> 00:01:33.566
edited down a little
bit for podcast purposes

00:01:33.566 --> 00:01:35.906
and it's safe to consume
while you're driving

00:01:35.906 --> 00:01:38.386
or doing other things that
you might need your eyes for.

00:01:38.386 --> 00:01:42.066
So check that out wherever
you find your podcasts.

00:01:42.066 --> 00:01:46.336
So with that, let's get
into the ask me anything.

00:01:46.336 --> 00:01:48.116
And there's one that we got a little ahead

00:01:48.116 --> 00:01:51.016
of time from Dolce Castro Garcia.

00:01:51.016 --> 00:01:53.626
This was actually I think on
the last one from Thursday,

00:01:53.626 --> 00:01:55.936
but I figured it's a good place to start.

00:01:55.936 --> 00:01:58.576
Dolce wrote, "Mr. Kahn, until this live,"

00:01:58.576 --> 00:01:59.883
I guess this live stream,

00:01:59.883 --> 00:02:01.936
"I didn't know who had made the platform

00:02:01.936 --> 00:02:04.306
I love working on so much.

00:02:04.306 --> 00:02:06.716
How did you get the idea
to make the platform

00:02:06.716 --> 00:02:07.696
or what inspired you?

00:02:07.696 --> 00:02:10.016
Thank you so much. I'll
make sure to donate."

00:02:10.016 --> 00:02:13.456
Well, Dolce, thank you
so much that you enjoy

00:02:13.456 --> 00:02:15.469
working on Khan Academy.

00:02:16.652 --> 00:02:20.216
A lot of folks talk about
people having a growth mindset

00:02:20.216 --> 00:02:21.246
and people with a growth mindset

00:02:21.246 --> 00:02:22.766
are constantly challenging themselves

00:02:22.766 --> 00:02:23.866
and pushing their boundaries

00:02:23.866 --> 00:02:25.306
and people with a growth mindset

00:02:25.306 --> 00:02:27.276
are also disproportionately
successful in life.

00:02:27.276 --> 00:02:28.796
And the fact that it sounds

00:02:28.796 --> 00:02:32.096
like you like going to Khan
Academy, challenging yourself,

00:02:32.096 --> 00:02:34.476
keep pushing the boundaries
of your knowledge,

00:02:34.476 --> 00:02:36.996
I think says a lot about you

00:02:36.996 --> 00:02:38.866
and hopefully will be a small part

00:02:38.866 --> 00:02:41.686
in you becoming an
incredibly empowered person.

00:02:41.686 --> 00:02:44.066
And thanks for the willingness to donate

00:02:44.066 --> 00:02:45.826
if you're in a position to do so.

00:02:45.826 --> 00:02:48.836
In terms of where the
inspiration for Khan Academy

00:02:48.836 --> 00:02:53.309
came from, you know, it
goes pretty far back.

00:02:54.466 --> 00:02:57.776
Khan Academy immediately started
getting worked on in 2004

00:02:57.776 --> 00:02:59.756
when one of my cousins needed help.

00:02:59.756 --> 00:03:02.076
I was an analyst at a
hedge fund at the time.

00:03:02.076 --> 00:03:04.616
My original background was
in math and computer science

00:03:04.616 --> 00:03:06.396
and I had worked in tech as well,

00:03:06.396 --> 00:03:07.766
but I started tutoring her,

00:03:07.766 --> 00:03:10.026
word spread through my family
free tutoring was going on.

00:03:10.026 --> 00:03:11.706
I soon found myself tutoring

00:03:11.706 --> 00:03:14.026
10/15 cousins around the country.

00:03:14.026 --> 00:03:16.216
I started writing software for them

00:03:16.216 --> 00:03:17.846
because I saw a common pattern

00:03:17.846 --> 00:03:19.966
is that they all had
gaps in their knowledge.

00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:22.536
And I wanted some ways
for them to practice

00:03:22.536 --> 00:03:23.576
and fill in those gaps.

00:03:23.576 --> 00:03:26.116
And for me as their
teacher or their tutor,

00:03:26.116 --> 00:03:27.736
to monitor what they were doing

00:03:27.736 --> 00:03:28.826
so that when we got on the phone,

00:03:28.826 --> 00:03:30.366
we could dig a little bit deeper.

00:03:30.366 --> 00:03:32.196
And that was the first Khan Academy,

00:03:32.196 --> 00:03:34.096
that exercise platform I wrote for them.

00:03:34.096 --> 00:03:35.976
And it actually still is
the core of Khan Academy,

00:03:35.976 --> 00:03:37.356
this notion to be able to practice

00:03:37.356 --> 00:03:40.756
and get as much feedback as you need.

00:03:40.756 --> 00:03:43.736
And then it was a friend
in 2006 who suggested

00:03:43.736 --> 00:03:46.796
that I make videos to
supplement the exercise portion

00:03:46.796 --> 00:03:50.256
of Khan Academy and
upload them onto YouTube.

00:03:50.256 --> 00:03:52.026
I thought it was a horrible idea.

00:03:52.026 --> 00:03:54.586
I told them YouTube is
for cats playing piano,

00:03:54.586 --> 00:03:56.976
not serious mathematics,

00:03:56.976 --> 00:03:59.836
but I got over the idea
that it wasn't my idea.

00:03:59.836 --> 00:04:01.266
And I gave it a shot.

00:04:01.266 --> 00:04:02.416
And you know,

00:04:02.416 --> 00:04:04.326
my cousins famously told
me they liked me better

00:04:04.326 --> 00:04:06.406
on YouTube than in-person.

00:04:06.406 --> 00:04:08.026
Take that how you will.

00:04:08.026 --> 00:04:09.566
What they were saying is
that they really appreciate

00:04:09.566 --> 00:04:11.306
having an on-demand version,

00:04:11.306 --> 00:04:12.936
an infinitely repeatable version

00:04:12.936 --> 00:04:14.086
of their cousin that they could watch

00:04:14.086 --> 00:04:15.376
in the middle of the night, no judgment,

00:04:15.376 --> 00:04:18.176
but they still enjoyed having
the phone calls with me.

00:04:18.176 --> 00:04:19.896
But, you know, and then
became clear people

00:04:19.896 --> 00:04:22.496
who are not my cousins
were using this resource

00:04:22.496 --> 00:04:25.996
and not just the software,
but the videos as well.

00:04:25.996 --> 00:04:28.826
By 2009, I had set up Khan Academy

00:04:28.826 --> 00:04:31.306
as a not-for-profit organization.

00:04:31.306 --> 00:04:32.666
To be clear what a not-for-profit is,

00:04:32.666 --> 00:04:34.016
no one owns a not-for-profit.

00:04:34.016 --> 00:04:35.186
I don't own Khan Academy.

00:04:35.186 --> 00:04:38.396
You own as much of Khan Academy
as I own of Khan Academy.

00:04:38.396 --> 00:04:41.296
None of us own, it's really for society.

00:04:41.296 --> 00:04:43.156
But I set it up as a
not-for-profit and I said,

00:04:43.156 --> 00:04:46.436
well, the social return
on investment on this,

00:04:46.436 --> 00:04:48.096
the social impact could
be through the roof.

00:04:48.096 --> 00:04:48.996
I set it up with the mission

00:04:48.996 --> 00:04:51.326
of free world class education
for anyone anywhere.

00:04:51.326 --> 00:04:52.446
And even though at that time

00:04:52.446 --> 00:04:54.396
there were about 50 or a
100 thousand folks using it,

00:04:54.396 --> 00:04:56.126
I imagined and I was operating

00:04:56.126 --> 00:04:58.196
out of the same closet where I am now,

00:04:58.196 --> 00:05:00.156
that maybe one day it could be millions,

00:05:00.156 --> 00:05:02.766
10 millions, hundreds of
millions, billions of folks.

00:05:02.766 --> 00:05:04.316
And so I quit my day job

00:05:05.626 --> 00:05:08.046
and tried to find
philanthropic funding for it.

00:05:08.046 --> 00:05:10.786
And that first year was a very tough year.

00:05:10.786 --> 00:05:12.906
I think anytime you do
anything entrepreneurial,

00:05:12.906 --> 00:05:14.856
you almost have to have
that delusional optimism,

00:05:14.856 --> 00:05:16.386
assume that people are gonna step up

00:05:16.386 --> 00:05:19.053
and realize what you think you realize

00:05:19.053 --> 00:05:22.376
but it always takes a little
bit more time than you suspect.

00:05:22.376 --> 00:05:25.236
And so it was actually very,
very stressful that 2009.

00:05:26.136 --> 00:05:27.546
Our first child had just been born,

00:05:27.546 --> 00:05:29.316
our expenses went up,

00:05:29.316 --> 00:05:31.616
my wife was still in
training to be a physician.

00:05:31.616 --> 00:05:33.326
So it was hard.

00:05:33.326 --> 00:05:35.806
But as we got kind of month 9, month 10,

00:05:35.806 --> 00:05:36.786
then some philanthropists

00:05:36.786 --> 00:05:38.406
started to come out of the woodwork.

00:05:38.406 --> 00:05:41.356
And then by that fall,
some major foundations,

00:05:41.356 --> 00:05:44.186
Google.org, the Gates
Foundation stepped up

00:05:44.186 --> 00:05:45.846
for us to become a real organization.

00:05:45.846 --> 00:05:47.706
So that was really the
genesis of Khan Academy

00:05:47.706 --> 00:05:50.646
and it's been a ongoing ride ever since.

00:05:50.646 --> 00:05:52.356
And it's all because of support

00:05:52.356 --> 00:05:55.196
from both the large
foundations and corporations

00:05:55.196 --> 00:05:57.956
and folks like yourself
that make this possible

00:05:57.956 --> 00:06:00.376
because right now we're
only really throttled back

00:06:00.376 --> 00:06:02.406
by how much resources we can apply

00:06:02.406 --> 00:06:05.536
to this problem that
affects all of humanity.

00:06:05.536 --> 00:06:09.846
But thanks for that question,
Dolce, I think was your name.

00:06:09.846 --> 00:06:13.233
And then let's see, YouTube, Anuj Pagwan,

00:06:13.233 --> 00:06:17.526
"You can be a billionaire
but what you are doing free,

00:06:17.526 --> 00:06:19.964
what is your source of energy?"

00:06:19.964 --> 00:06:22.776
Anuj, well, I guess you
were making illusion

00:06:22.776 --> 00:06:25.316
to like maybe if Khan
Academy was for-profit,

00:06:25.316 --> 00:06:28.056
I would own some of it
and it could go public

00:06:28.056 --> 00:06:29.889
and maybe be worth billions, maybe.

00:06:30.966 --> 00:06:33.996
I did the thought experiment
back when I was deciding

00:06:33.996 --> 00:06:36.296
whether to make Khan Academy
for-profit or nonprofit,

00:06:36.296 --> 00:06:37.596
and I live in Silicon Valley.

00:06:37.596 --> 00:06:39.526
And so there were some venture capitalists

00:06:39.526 --> 00:06:41.463
who were reaching out
at the time and saying,

00:06:41.463 --> 00:06:43.266
"Hey, our kids use Khan Academy.

00:06:43.266 --> 00:06:44.366
We think you're onto something.

00:06:44.366 --> 00:06:45.906
We'll write a quick
check for you right now.

00:06:45.906 --> 00:06:48.186
You can quit your job, start doing it.

00:06:48.186 --> 00:06:49.019
And we think this could be

00:06:49.019 --> 00:06:51.796
a really successful ed
tech for-profit company."

00:06:51.796 --> 00:06:54.896
And it was tempting because I
could immediately work on it.

00:06:54.896 --> 00:06:57.456
Maybe if it's successful,
I could be a billionaire

00:06:57.456 --> 00:07:00.259
which, you know, I could
dress better, et cetera.

00:07:01.296 --> 00:07:02.216
But then when I really thought

00:07:02.216 --> 00:07:05.439
about what would I do
with a billion dollars,

00:07:07.666 --> 00:07:11.286
yes, I would probably maybe
dress a little bit better.

00:07:11.286 --> 00:07:13.386
I probably wouldn't even dress any better.

00:07:15.268 --> 00:07:16.386
I have everything I need.

00:07:16.386 --> 00:07:18.556
Even back then I kinda
had everything I need.

00:07:18.556 --> 00:07:21.226
I have a wonderful family,

00:07:21.226 --> 00:07:24.806
a smart, beautiful,
amazing, intelligent wife.

00:07:24.806 --> 00:07:27.596
I have now, at the time I had one son,

00:07:27.596 --> 00:07:29.306
but now I have two sons and a daughter.

00:07:29.306 --> 00:07:30.626
They're incredibly fun

00:07:30.626 --> 00:07:35.406
and give me incredible
energy and contentment.

00:07:35.406 --> 00:07:37.906
And you know, if you have those things

00:07:37.906 --> 00:07:41.186
and if you have enough
resources to pay your mortgage,

00:07:41.186 --> 00:07:43.196
go on vacations every now and then,

00:07:43.196 --> 00:07:45.346
go to a restaurant every now and then,

00:07:45.346 --> 00:07:47.486
so you're not struggling financially,

00:07:47.486 --> 00:07:49.506
which you know, growing up,

00:07:49.506 --> 00:07:50.936
my mom raised us by herself

00:07:50.936 --> 00:07:52.216
and she worked a lot of minimum wage jobs.

00:07:52.216 --> 00:07:54.956
So I know how hard it is when
you struggle financially.

00:07:54.956 --> 00:07:56.506
But if you're not struggling financially,

00:07:56.506 --> 00:07:58.396
if you have your base necessities,

00:07:58.396 --> 00:08:03.156
we have two Hondas,
what else could I want?

00:08:03.156 --> 00:08:06.006
And Honda doesn't even pay
me, I just keep buying Hondas.

00:08:07.906 --> 00:08:10.126
I'm not trying to be some type
of social media influencer,

00:08:10.126 --> 00:08:13.156
I've just always been
kind of a fan of Hondas,

00:08:13.156 --> 00:08:14.666
they haven't let me down.

00:08:14.666 --> 00:08:16.456
But I was like, what would
I do with more money?

00:08:16.456 --> 00:08:18.486
I would probably just donate it all

00:08:18.486 --> 00:08:20.306
to an effort like Khan Academy.

00:08:20.306 --> 00:08:22.876
And so why why not
even, why wait for that,

00:08:22.876 --> 00:08:24.156
just do it in reverse.

00:08:24.156 --> 00:08:25.806
Do the philanthropy in reverse.

00:08:25.806 --> 00:08:27.736
Just go straight to the not-for-profit

00:08:27.736 --> 00:08:29.616
and then try to find other folks

00:08:29.616 --> 00:08:31.456
who are aligned to help fund it.

00:08:31.456 --> 00:08:33.656
And, you know, I do
collect a salary from it.

00:08:33.656 --> 00:08:37.526
Everyone at Khan Academy,
we're paid to do what we do,

00:08:37.526 --> 00:08:38.896
but obviously we're not paid in stock.

00:08:38.896 --> 00:08:42.566
We're not gazillionaires and
billionaires and all of that,

00:08:42.566 --> 00:08:45.726
but I'm very happy with that
trade off personally in life.

00:08:45.726 --> 00:08:48.896
We had Francis Ford Coppola
on several months ago

00:08:48.896 --> 00:08:51.046
and he brought up this
term called, you know,

00:08:51.046 --> 00:08:53.316
in life, a lot of people are
trying to become millionaires

00:08:53.316 --> 00:08:55.346
or millionaires are trying
to become billionaires

00:08:55.346 --> 00:08:56.836
but he coined a new term
called sufficientaire.

00:08:56.836 --> 00:08:58.826
And that's when you have enough.

00:08:58.826 --> 00:09:02.486
And if in your mind
you're a sufficientaire,

00:09:02.486 --> 00:09:04.056
you're actually the wealthiest of all,

00:09:04.056 --> 00:09:05.686
because I know people
who are probably worth

00:09:05.686 --> 00:09:07.266
a billion who wanna be worth two billion

00:09:07.266 --> 00:09:08.876
and they're kind of waking
up in the middle of the night

00:09:08.876 --> 00:09:10.806
like, how am I gonna
make the next billion?

00:09:10.806 --> 00:09:13.676
But if you're sufficientaire,
then you have enough.

00:09:13.676 --> 00:09:16.523
And I consider myself a sufficientaire.

00:09:16.523 --> 00:09:21.523
I have hopefully a reasonable
financial security,

00:09:22.506 --> 00:09:26.146
have all what I need, and
I get to work on a mission

00:09:26.146 --> 00:09:29.676
that I care about, I get to
work with incredible people,

00:09:29.676 --> 00:09:33.046
I get to do goofy things like
these AMAs every now and then

00:09:33.046 --> 00:09:36.736
as part of my job, it's
intellectually challenging.

00:09:36.736 --> 00:09:40.739
So what else could I ask for?
So I have no regrets at all.

00:09:41.856 --> 00:09:44.613
So let's see, from YouTube,
Cade Heppelmann says,

00:09:44.613 --> 00:09:47.263
"What makes you most proud
of with Khan Academy?"

00:09:49.636 --> 00:09:52.656
You know, when I meet
folks either virtually

00:09:52.656 --> 00:09:53.716
or I meet 'em in-person

00:09:53.716 --> 00:09:56.266
and they say that it's had
an impact on their life.

00:09:57.116 --> 00:09:59.036
I've gone through airport security

00:09:59.036 --> 00:10:02.776
and the person who's
walking me into the scanner

00:10:02.776 --> 00:10:05.126
all of a sudden says,
"Hey, you look familiar."

00:10:06.223 --> 00:10:07.163
And then they hear me talk, they're like,

00:10:07.163 --> 00:10:09.416
"You sound a lot like
that Khan Academy guy."

00:10:09.416 --> 00:10:12.711
And I'm like, well, "I
am the Khan Academy guy."

00:10:12.711 --> 00:10:17.711
And then they'll start talking
about how it helped them,

00:10:17.926 --> 00:10:19.166
how it got them through high school

00:10:19.166 --> 00:10:20.416
or how it got them through college.

00:10:20.416 --> 00:10:25.416
And it feels really good to
have that type of feedback,

00:10:26.356 --> 00:10:27.556
to hear those stories

00:10:27.556 --> 00:10:29.586
when you're doing an
otherwise not pleasant thing,

00:10:29.586 --> 00:10:31.736
like trying to check
into airport security.

00:10:33.363 --> 00:10:34.576
And we get letters all of the time

00:10:34.576 --> 00:10:36.986
and that really is what
gives me a lot of energy

00:10:36.986 --> 00:10:38.286
and gives me a lot of satisfaction.

00:10:38.286 --> 00:10:39.119
And that you know,

00:10:39.119 --> 00:10:41.406
I tell this to the Khan
Academy team all the time,

00:10:41.406 --> 00:10:42.926
these are the stories we hear about,

00:10:42.926 --> 00:10:44.666
but we know that there's tens of millions

00:10:44.666 --> 00:10:46.226
of folks on the platform.

00:10:46.226 --> 00:10:49.676
So that there's literally stadiums full

00:10:49.676 --> 00:10:51.496
of people right now who are learning,

00:10:51.496 --> 00:10:52.956
who are tapping into their potential

00:10:52.956 --> 00:10:54.496
because of Khan Academy that might not

00:10:54.496 --> 00:10:55.896
have otherwise been able to.

00:10:56.876 --> 00:10:58.036
And for me, I just think

00:10:58.036 --> 00:11:01.396
about all of that human
potential that is growing

00:11:01.396 --> 00:11:02.326
and growing and growing.

00:11:02.326 --> 00:11:05.106
I remember reading a story
about Warren Buffett.

00:11:05.106 --> 00:11:07.256
This was well before
I started Khan Academy

00:11:07.256 --> 00:11:09.796
but they asked him about his investment

00:11:09.796 --> 00:11:12.076
in Gillette, which makes shaving razors.

00:11:12.076 --> 00:11:14.246
And he says, "I sleep very well at night

00:11:14.246 --> 00:11:16.176
just imagining all the hair growing

00:11:16.176 --> 00:11:18.376
on all the men's faces around the world.

00:11:18.376 --> 00:11:21.006
And that's just going to drive
the profits of Gillette."

00:11:21.006 --> 00:11:23.506
Well, my analog is I go to sleep

00:11:23.506 --> 00:11:25.236
and I just imagine all of the people

00:11:25.236 --> 00:11:26.836
who are not the hair is growing

00:11:26.836 --> 00:11:28.336
but their intellect is growing.

00:11:28.336 --> 00:11:31.286
Their neurons are connecting
because of Khan Academy.

00:11:31.286 --> 00:11:33.726
And hopefully their self-esteem is growing

00:11:33.726 --> 00:11:35.486
because they're able to
tap into their potential.

00:11:35.486 --> 00:11:38.506
And I don't think there's
anything more exciting than that.

00:11:38.506 --> 00:11:42.756
And selfishly, all of you who
are growing intellectually

00:11:42.756 --> 00:11:44.626
and your self-esteem and
your confidence is growing,

00:11:44.626 --> 00:11:45.996
you're gonna do amazing things.

00:11:45.996 --> 00:11:47.676
Many of those things are going to benefit

00:11:47.676 --> 00:11:48.826
all of us, including me.

00:11:48.826 --> 00:11:49.976
So it'll all come back.

00:11:49.976 --> 00:11:51.066
One of y'all are gonna find a cure

00:11:51.066 --> 00:11:55.870
for some disease or solve
climate change or part of it,

00:11:55.870 --> 00:11:58.076
or write the next great novel.

00:11:58.076 --> 00:11:59.576
And hopefully if Khan Academy plays

00:11:59.576 --> 00:12:02.169
a little small part of
it, that'd be incredible.

00:12:03.476 --> 00:12:05.513
Let's see, from Facebook, Patrick Breslin,

00:12:05.513 --> 00:12:08.076
"How can we improve the online
learning classroom experience

00:12:08.076 --> 00:12:10.343
ideas and improvements
in 2020 and beyond?"

00:12:11.866 --> 00:12:13.226
Well, there's some high-level things

00:12:13.226 --> 00:12:15.630
that actually don't need technology.

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:19.566
I think the interactive
live video conference

00:12:19.566 --> 00:12:22.636
is crucial especially if
you're doing distance learning.

00:12:22.636 --> 00:12:25.316
Now, to be clear, I don't
think distance learning,

00:12:25.316 --> 00:12:27.226
100% distance learning is not optimal

00:12:27.226 --> 00:12:29.986
for most people, including for myself.

00:12:29.986 --> 00:12:31.616
You need to be in a classroom,

00:12:31.616 --> 00:12:33.986
interact with people, see body language.

00:12:33.986 --> 00:12:36.566
That stuff is super powerful.

00:12:36.566 --> 00:12:37.566
I mean, obviously during COVID,

00:12:37.566 --> 00:12:40.366
we have to do distance
learning and then beyond COVID,

00:12:40.366 --> 00:12:42.356
there could be spaces for
it where it gives you access

00:12:42.356 --> 00:12:43.766
to courses that might not be available

00:12:43.766 --> 00:12:45.846
in your school or connected to students

00:12:45.846 --> 00:12:48.756
who you might not otherwise
be able to connect with.

00:12:48.756 --> 00:12:51.086
But whenever you're
doing anything distanced,

00:12:51.086 --> 00:12:52.306
especially video conference,

00:12:52.306 --> 00:12:54.376
you gotta make it as
interactive as possible.

00:12:54.376 --> 00:12:56.016
I mean, if I just lecture
to y'all right now

00:12:56.016 --> 00:12:58.036
without looking at your questions,

00:12:58.036 --> 00:12:59.776
it might as well be a video.

00:12:59.776 --> 00:13:03.506
And ideally, if we're all really
live on a video conference,

00:13:03.506 --> 00:13:05.626
I should be asking you all questions

00:13:05.626 --> 00:13:07.276
and seeing your responses,

00:13:07.276 --> 00:13:09.256
putting you into breakout sessions,

00:13:09.256 --> 00:13:10.866
being creative on the use of time.

00:13:10.866 --> 00:13:12.986
Instead of 60 minutes with 30 kids,

00:13:12.986 --> 00:13:17.986
why not six 10-minute
sessions with five kids each

00:13:18.926 --> 00:13:20.706
so you can get a little
bit more personalization.

00:13:20.706 --> 00:13:22.526
Break it up every now and
then with just fun activities,

00:13:22.526 --> 00:13:25.266
close your eyes and draw an elephant

00:13:25.266 --> 00:13:26.246
and then share it with everyone.

00:13:26.246 --> 00:13:27.646
Maybe in a breakout session.

00:13:27.646 --> 00:13:28.596
Those are the types of things

00:13:28.596 --> 00:13:31.376
that when we see are done
well, make it really engaging.

00:13:31.376 --> 00:13:33.706
And frankly, everything I just
said are also good practices

00:13:33.706 --> 00:13:34.656
in a physical classroom,

00:13:34.656 --> 00:13:37.569
make it as interactive as
possible, human to human.

00:13:40.526 --> 00:13:43.413
From YouTube, Hari Aburu says,

00:13:43.413 --> 00:13:45.666
"How did you manage to
upload millions of videos,

00:13:45.666 --> 00:13:47.026
helping millions of people?"

00:13:47.026 --> 00:13:48.416
Well, Hari, to be clear,

00:13:48.416 --> 00:13:51.069
Khan Academy is much more than just me.

00:13:52.744 --> 00:13:54.316
And I haven't uploaded millions of videos.

00:13:54.316 --> 00:13:56.236
Personally, I have made
thousands of videos.

00:13:56.236 --> 00:13:59.026
I think it's probably be about
6,000 or 7,000 at this point.

00:13:59.026 --> 00:14:00.776
And it seems like a lot,

00:14:00.776 --> 00:14:03.386
but when you look at how many
days in the year there are

00:14:03.386 --> 00:14:06.739
times I've been making videos
for about 14 years now,

00:14:08.076 --> 00:14:10.199
yeah, you can get several thousand,

00:14:11.326 --> 00:14:14.096
if you think about, yeah,

00:14:14.096 --> 00:14:16.796
if you say roughly over
10 years 7,000 videos,

00:14:16.796 --> 00:14:17.629
I mean, I've been doing it

00:14:17.629 --> 00:14:19.476
for a little bit more than 10 years,

00:14:19.476 --> 00:14:22.026
you're looking at about 700 videos a year.

00:14:22.026 --> 00:14:24.736
If you think I've been doing
it for about 12, 13, 14 years,

00:14:24.736 --> 00:14:28.016
it's a little bit lower,
maybe 500/600 videos a year.

00:14:28.016 --> 00:14:31.716
And so that's averaging about,
there's about 250 work days,

00:14:31.716 --> 00:14:34.826
so I'm averaging about two
to five videos per workday.

00:14:34.826 --> 00:14:36.326
And I still make videos,
I made one this morning.

00:14:36.326 --> 00:14:38.756
I still haven't uploaded yet, it's coming.

00:14:38.756 --> 00:14:40.516
It's Simpson's Diversity Index

00:14:40.516 --> 00:14:45.066
in biology of how do you quantify
diversity in a population.

00:14:45.066 --> 00:14:47.993
Let's see. So Hari asked another question.

00:14:47.993 --> 00:14:51.193
"What do you think is the
hardest topic in mathematics?"

00:14:53.656 --> 00:14:56.466
You know, what I found is
any topic in mathematics,

00:14:56.466 --> 00:14:58.326
it can sometimes seem hard at first,

00:14:58.326 --> 00:14:59.756
'cause it's kind of
stretching your thinking,

00:14:59.756 --> 00:15:02.026
it's making you visualize
things in a different way,

00:15:02.026 --> 00:15:02.859
or it might even be things

00:15:02.859 --> 00:15:04.536
that you find trouble visualizing.

00:15:04.536 --> 00:15:06.306
Like, I remember the first time,

00:15:06.306 --> 00:15:07.706
you know, in linear algebra,

00:15:09.166 --> 00:15:10.996
you start generalizing this notion

00:15:10.996 --> 00:15:13.246
of two-dimensional, three-dimensional,

00:15:13.246 --> 00:15:14.446
and then you go to N-space

00:15:14.446 --> 00:15:17.466
that you can have an arbitrary
number of dimensions.

00:15:17.466 --> 00:15:19.666
And then you can essentially
do linear algebra

00:15:19.666 --> 00:15:22.156
within those arbitrary
number of dimensions.

00:15:22.156 --> 00:15:23.826
And mathematically, you can come up

00:15:23.826 --> 00:15:25.406
with some really interesting conclusions

00:15:25.406 --> 00:15:27.496
about things or insights about things.

00:15:27.496 --> 00:15:29.586
And at first, I remember thinking,

00:15:29.586 --> 00:15:31.746
how do I think beyond three dimensions?

00:15:31.746 --> 00:15:33.396
I mean, maybe we can
think in four dimensions

00:15:33.396 --> 00:15:35.356
if you include time, but you know,

00:15:35.356 --> 00:15:38.316
the more you get familiar with
it, your brain, it is true,

00:15:38.316 --> 00:15:43.316
the human brain cannot really
visualize four dimensions

00:15:44.306 --> 00:15:46.536
or definitely not five dimensions,

00:15:46.536 --> 00:15:49.016
but you can start to
think about how it works

00:15:49.016 --> 00:15:53.616
and actually the math can help
you think about what happens.

00:15:53.616 --> 00:15:55.426
What would it be like to experience

00:15:55.426 --> 00:15:58.056
if we had four spatial dimensions

00:15:58.056 --> 00:16:01.246
or five spatial dimensions
or things like that.

00:16:01.246 --> 00:16:04.346
So, you know, I think,
and there's other topics

00:16:04.346 --> 00:16:06.719
in mathematics that can be very abstract.

00:16:08.416 --> 00:16:10.796
It might seem very esoteric
but what I found is the more,

00:16:10.796 --> 00:16:12.356
in any subject, not just math,

00:16:12.356 --> 00:16:14.126
that you really apply yourself to it,

00:16:14.126 --> 00:16:17.366
you allow your subconscious
to ponder a little bit,

00:16:17.366 --> 00:16:18.616
give yourself some time and space,

00:16:18.616 --> 00:16:21.276
at some point those
connections are gonna happen.

00:16:21.276 --> 00:16:22.996
And I'm actually convinced anything

00:16:22.996 --> 00:16:25.426
that is understandable
by any other human being

00:16:25.426 --> 00:16:28.056
is actually understandable
by any other human being

00:16:28.056 --> 00:16:30.106
just given enough time.

00:16:30.106 --> 00:16:31.526
If you go back a thousand years,

00:16:31.526 --> 00:16:33.156
things like algebra were considered

00:16:33.156 --> 00:16:37.596
like super advanced, like
only the elite of the elite

00:16:37.596 --> 00:16:40.736
mathematicians would, you know,

00:16:40.736 --> 00:16:44.266
al-Khwarizmi famously is the
one who really invented algebra

00:16:44.266 --> 00:16:48.736
and algebra is, you know,
algebra comes from Arabic,

00:16:48.736 --> 00:16:51.246
and this was kind of in the golden age

00:16:51.246 --> 00:16:53.936
of kind of Islamic mathematics,

00:16:53.936 --> 00:16:56.736
I think it coincides with the
famous library in Baghdad.

00:16:56.736 --> 00:16:58.806
So back then, for much of the world,

00:16:58.806 --> 00:17:01.556
this was like super advanced stuff.

00:17:01.556 --> 00:17:03.956
I think this was in the
11th or 12th century,

00:17:03.956 --> 00:17:05.306
but you fast forward today,

00:17:07.326 --> 00:17:09.576
you start learning algebra in sixth grade,

00:17:09.576 --> 00:17:11.846
11 or 12 years old and you
really are supposed to master

00:17:11.846 --> 00:17:13.106
algebra or at least the core of it

00:17:13.106 --> 00:17:15.376
by the time you're in 9th or 10th grade.

00:17:15.376 --> 00:17:19.976
So I think even things like
tensor analysis or typology,

00:17:19.976 --> 00:17:21.996
or you know, I could come up

00:17:21.996 --> 00:17:23.066
with some of these abstract things.

00:17:23.066 --> 00:17:26.426
I think over time, they're gonna
become more and more common

00:17:26.426 --> 00:17:30.146
for more and more people to
really understand these things.

00:17:30.146 --> 00:17:32.493
So from YouTube, Sudheer Yadav says,

00:17:32.493 --> 00:17:34.026
"How could I balance my life

00:17:34.026 --> 00:17:37.496
between monthly exams and what
I really wanna do in life?"

00:17:37.496 --> 00:17:38.329
Good question.

00:17:38.329 --> 00:17:40.306
And I would say the key is balance,

00:17:40.306 --> 00:17:41.966
no matter what stage of life you're at,

00:17:41.966 --> 00:17:43.976
whether you're a student and
you have to take your exams

00:17:43.976 --> 00:17:46.436
and study for them or
whether you're working

00:17:46.436 --> 00:17:48.836
and you have some passions
you wanna work on.

00:17:48.836 --> 00:17:50.976
I think if you really
determine yourself, you say,

00:17:50.976 --> 00:17:53.486
look I am going to set
aside X hours a day.

00:17:53.486 --> 00:17:54.616
Maybe it's only an hour a day,

00:17:54.616 --> 00:17:56.846
two hours, or maybe it's only three hours

00:17:56.846 --> 00:18:00.106
on the weekend to do
this thing I care about.

00:18:00.106 --> 00:18:01.846
You should be able to do it.

00:18:01.846 --> 00:18:03.956
There's a lot of stuff we do
when we're procrastinating,

00:18:03.956 --> 00:18:07.046
not efficient use of time
that when we really think

00:18:07.046 --> 00:18:08.836
about it and reflect on it, we're like,

00:18:08.836 --> 00:18:10.636
it really doesn't make us feel good.

00:18:11.476 --> 00:18:13.156
Let me focus that energy on the stuff

00:18:13.156 --> 00:18:14.716
that I really wanna work on.

00:18:14.716 --> 00:18:17.516
And, you know I say this 'cause
Khan Academy wouldn't exist.

00:18:17.516 --> 00:18:19.286
Y'all heard the story of how it started.

00:18:19.286 --> 00:18:21.096
If I said I'm a hedge fund analyst,

00:18:21.096 --> 00:18:22.256
that's all I'm gonna do,

00:18:22.256 --> 00:18:24.446
my cousin needs help but
I'm gonna ignore her.

00:18:24.446 --> 00:18:26.987
Khan Academy exists because I said, look,

00:18:26.987 --> 00:18:29.626
this is important to me,
I'm interested in tutoring,

00:18:29.626 --> 00:18:31.166
I wanna help my cousin

00:18:31.166 --> 00:18:33.616
and I wanna write software,
I wanna help them.

00:18:33.616 --> 00:18:36.469
And I'm curious about the
broader problem of education.

00:18:37.306 --> 00:18:39.056
Let me carve out some time for this.

00:18:39.056 --> 00:18:40.456
And you know, when you do that,

00:18:40.456 --> 00:18:43.306
you think it might affect your day job,

00:18:43.306 --> 00:18:44.626
but in a lot of ways it doesn't,

00:18:44.626 --> 00:18:46.296
and it actually makes you
better at your day job,

00:18:46.296 --> 00:18:47.436
because then when you go to your day job,

00:18:47.436 --> 00:18:49.326
you're more energized, recharged,

00:18:49.326 --> 00:18:51.376
and you're used to thinking
in kind of different ways.

00:18:51.376 --> 00:18:53.946
So even now, there's a million
things I wanna get done

00:18:53.946 --> 00:18:56.356
with Khan Academy and
when Friday afternoon

00:18:56.356 --> 00:18:57.406
or evening comes around and I'm like,

00:18:57.406 --> 00:18:58.966
oh, maybe I should just
work through the weekend

00:18:58.966 --> 00:19:00.716
and crank that stuff out.

00:19:00.716 --> 00:19:02.586
But I remind myself that if I do that,

00:19:02.586 --> 00:19:05.326
I'm just going to deplete
myself and actually probably

00:19:05.326 --> 00:19:08.196
make me less effective in
my role at Khan Academy.

00:19:08.196 --> 00:19:11.596
And that I should invest that time,

00:19:11.596 --> 00:19:13.576
be present for my own needs,

00:19:13.576 --> 00:19:16.599
for my wife's needs,
for my children's needs.

00:19:17.937 --> 00:19:21.476
So that, one, it's for
them, but also for myself.

00:19:21.476 --> 00:19:24.126
I think no one on their death bed wishes

00:19:24.126 --> 00:19:26.926
that they had worked for
their job a little bit harder.

00:19:26.926 --> 00:19:28.476
Everyone on their death bed, I think,

00:19:28.476 --> 00:19:31.176
or a lot of folks when on their deathbed,

00:19:31.176 --> 00:19:32.766
hopefully a long time from now, they say,

00:19:32.766 --> 00:19:34.506
oh, I wish I spent more
time with my family.

00:19:34.506 --> 00:19:36.981
I wish I invested more in my passions.

00:19:36.981 --> 00:19:41.606
I wish when my kids were young,
I really appreciated them.

00:19:41.606 --> 00:19:43.636
I look at videos now of, you know,

00:19:43.636 --> 00:19:45.996
my kids are 11, 9 and 6.

00:19:45.996 --> 00:19:48.366
And I look at videos from
even four or five years ago

00:19:48.366 --> 00:19:50.516
when they were younger and I was like,

00:19:50.516 --> 00:19:52.366
man, they were these
little chunky monkeys,

00:19:52.366 --> 00:19:53.746
how come I didn't squeeze them all day?

00:19:53.746 --> 00:19:55.876
And then I remember how
tiring it was as well,

00:19:55.876 --> 00:19:58.146
so I was probably just tired.

00:19:58.146 --> 00:20:00.016
But I remind myself that in five years

00:20:00.016 --> 00:20:02.276
when I see videos of my own kids today,

00:20:02.276 --> 00:20:04.096
I'm gonna be like, oh, look
how cute and adorable they were

00:20:04.096 --> 00:20:05.749
and they were so fun and curious.

00:20:06.714 --> 00:20:08.476
How come you ignored them
when you were trying to write

00:20:08.476 --> 00:20:11.119
that memo to whatever whatever.

00:20:12.346 --> 00:20:13.906
And so I just remind myself all the time

00:20:13.906 --> 00:20:15.006
it's all about balance

00:20:16.211 --> 00:20:21.146
and really prioritizing your
inner self, your family,

00:20:21.146 --> 00:20:23.926
and your friendships so
that they don't get lost

00:20:23.926 --> 00:20:26.946
and it's not gonna hurt your
other things you have to do

00:20:26.946 --> 00:20:28.879
whether it's academics or a job.

00:20:29.756 --> 00:20:34.052
So let's see, YouTube,
Susana Garcia Dominguez.

00:20:34.052 --> 00:20:36.256
I don't wanna have
favorite question askers,

00:20:36.256 --> 00:20:39.656
but Susanna has consistently
ask some very good questions.

00:20:39.656 --> 00:20:42.706
So not to make all of y'all feel bad.

00:20:42.706 --> 00:20:43.976
I haven't seen the
question she's gonna ask.

00:20:43.976 --> 00:20:46.776
She says, "Hello Sal, thank
you for having Homeroom today.

00:20:46.776 --> 00:20:49.036
I was wondering about MIT.

00:20:49.036 --> 00:20:50.236
Do they have a big endowment?

00:20:50.236 --> 00:20:51.816
What scholarships are best to apply for?

00:20:51.816 --> 00:20:53.926
What is the best way to fund MIT?"

00:20:53.926 --> 00:20:57.356
I'm assuming fund as a
student, if you wanna go.

00:20:57.356 --> 00:21:00.686
'Cause MIT is a reasonably
wealthy university.

00:21:00.686 --> 00:21:02.576
I don't know what their
endowment is today,

00:21:02.576 --> 00:21:04.376
but it's in the billions of dollars.

00:21:05.519 --> 00:21:07.716
So, but you know, they do great things.

00:21:07.716 --> 00:21:09.876
So if anyone's thinking about funding MIT,

00:21:09.876 --> 00:21:11.706
they do some incredible research.

00:21:11.706 --> 00:21:13.286
So I'm a big fan.

00:21:13.286 --> 00:21:14.986
That's where I went to college.

00:21:14.986 --> 00:21:17.606
I'm guessing that's why Susanna
is asking me about that.

00:21:17.606 --> 00:21:20.276
But what scholarships are
the best to apply for?

00:21:20.276 --> 00:21:21.876
What is the best way to fund?

00:21:21.876 --> 00:21:23.646
I'm assuming going to MIT.

00:21:23.646 --> 00:21:27.356
So, you know, when I was in high school,

00:21:27.356 --> 00:21:29.136
my mom didn't make a lot of money.

00:21:29.136 --> 00:21:32.426
I think she was making,
I think $5 an hour,

00:21:32.426 --> 00:21:35.076
which even in the 1990s
was not a lot of money,

00:21:35.076 --> 00:21:36.606
it was minimum wage.

00:21:36.606 --> 00:21:40.286
And my older sister actually
applied to Brown University

00:21:40.286 --> 00:21:41.816
which is another very good university,

00:21:41.816 --> 00:21:42.649
Ivy League university.

00:21:42.649 --> 00:21:43.806
And I remember when she was applying,

00:21:43.806 --> 00:21:45.276
I was like, even if you
get in, how are we gonna?

00:21:45.276 --> 00:21:46.376
We can't afford going there.

00:21:46.376 --> 00:21:49.756
At the time, I think Brown's
tuition was $20,000 a year

00:21:49.756 --> 00:21:52.616
which was more than my
mother made in a year.

00:21:52.616 --> 00:21:53.896
And then room and board

00:21:53.896 --> 00:21:55.696
would have been another $10,000 a year.

00:21:55.696 --> 00:21:57.866
So back then it would have
been about $30,000 a year.

00:21:57.866 --> 00:22:01.759
Now, these places are
$50,000/$60,000 a year in some cases.

00:22:03.136 --> 00:22:03.993
And then my sister told me,

00:22:03.993 --> 00:22:06.636
"No, Sal. There's this
thing called financial aid."

00:22:06.636 --> 00:22:09.206
And if you get into some
of these universities,

00:22:09.206 --> 00:22:10.326
not all universities have them,

00:22:10.326 --> 00:22:11.666
but universities like Brown,

00:22:11.666 --> 00:22:14.766
universities like MIT that have
reasonable size endowments,

00:22:14.766 --> 00:22:16.496
they first of all try to be need-blind

00:22:16.496 --> 00:22:17.646
or I think they are need-blind

00:22:17.646 --> 00:22:18.976
when they look at an applicant.

00:22:18.976 --> 00:22:20.916
So they're not looking at you

00:22:20.916 --> 00:22:22.516
whether you're gonna
be able to pay or not.

00:22:22.516 --> 00:22:25.606
And then if you get
admitted, they try to make it

00:22:25.606 --> 00:22:26.986
so that you can pay.

00:22:26.986 --> 00:22:29.056
And actually, and so when I was...

00:22:29.056 --> 00:22:30.566
My sister was able to...

00:22:31.996 --> 00:22:33.556
She had a good bit of loans,

00:22:33.556 --> 00:22:37.006
but then she also had a
lot of grants from school

00:22:37.006 --> 00:22:39.416
and then she was able to go.

00:22:39.416 --> 00:22:40.646
And so that made me realize,

00:22:40.646 --> 00:22:41.479
oh wait maybe I could also think about it,

00:22:41.479 --> 00:22:43.436
'cause it was always my dream

00:22:43.436 --> 00:22:45.756
when I was young to go to MIT.

00:22:45.756 --> 00:22:46.589
A little bit arbitrary,

00:22:46.589 --> 00:22:47.726
I remember when I was seven years old,

00:22:47.726 --> 00:22:50.276
my uncle who was in his
twenties at the time

00:22:50.276 --> 00:22:51.676
at the University of New Orleans,

00:22:51.676 --> 00:22:53.726
he was a college student

00:22:53.726 --> 00:22:55.936
and he was doing his calculus homework.

00:22:55.936 --> 00:22:57.146
And I was like, "What is this?"

00:22:57.146 --> 00:22:59.226
He's like, "This is calculus."
I'm like, "What is calculus?"

00:22:59.226 --> 00:23:00.726
And he's like, "Well,
it's a little complex."

00:23:00.726 --> 00:23:02.796
I was like, "Explain it to
me." And then he explains it.

00:23:02.796 --> 00:23:04.796
And I was like, "Why are
you learning calculus?"

00:23:04.796 --> 00:23:06.296
He's like, "Well, I
wanna become an engineer.

00:23:06.296 --> 00:23:08.303
And then I'll be able to do
X Y, and Z, make things."

00:23:08.303 --> 00:23:10.346
And I said, "Then I wanna
become an engineer."

00:23:10.346 --> 00:23:12.616
And then he said, "Then
you should go to MIT."

00:23:12.616 --> 00:23:14.086
And he doesn't remember saying that

00:23:14.086 --> 00:23:17.156
but I remember saying,
"Okay, I will go to MIT."

00:23:17.156 --> 00:23:18.246
I didn't know what it stood for,

00:23:18.246 --> 00:23:19.806
I didn't know where it was

00:23:19.806 --> 00:23:22.726
but something clicked in my
brain at seven years old.

00:23:22.726 --> 00:23:24.176
And when I saw that my sister

00:23:24.176 --> 00:23:25.666
kind of pioneered that like, oh,

00:23:25.666 --> 00:23:27.386
there's this thing called financial aid,

00:23:27.386 --> 00:23:29.999
that gave me the confidence to apply.

00:23:31.025 --> 00:23:32.176
And even that I realized

00:23:32.176 --> 00:23:34.206
at the time was a little delusional.

00:23:34.206 --> 00:23:37.396
No one in my high school
had ever gone to MIT

00:23:37.396 --> 00:23:40.416
and they only were accepting
at the time five or six people

00:23:40.416 --> 00:23:42.536
from the state of Louisiana.

00:23:42.536 --> 00:23:45.346
I did not go to one of
those fancy schools,

00:23:45.346 --> 00:23:47.316
but somehow I got in.

00:23:47.316 --> 00:23:48.886
And they gave me a, I thought,

00:23:48.886 --> 00:23:50.596
a very generous financial aid package.

00:23:50.596 --> 00:23:51.626
So I did have debt.

00:23:51.626 --> 00:23:54.486
I had like $30,000 of
debt when I graduated

00:23:55.666 --> 00:23:58.096
but for the most part, all
the contribution came from me

00:23:58.096 --> 00:24:01.883
and my work study and my mom
didn't have to contribute much.

00:24:01.883 --> 00:24:04.206
And my understanding now
of a lot of these schools,

00:24:04.206 --> 00:24:08.126
if your family makes I believe
it's below $75,000 a year,

00:24:08.126 --> 00:24:10.496
it's essentially free.

00:24:10.496 --> 00:24:11.676
And I think in some cases,

00:24:11.676 --> 00:24:15.616
if your family makes below
125,000 or 150,000 a year,

00:24:15.616 --> 00:24:17.956
it's still heavily, heavily subsidized.

00:24:17.956 --> 00:24:19.296
So any of you all thinking about going,

00:24:19.296 --> 00:24:20.986
it's not just MIT or Brown,

00:24:20.986 --> 00:24:23.616
there's a lot of
universities, look into it,

00:24:23.616 --> 00:24:26.176
that give need-blind admissions

00:24:26.176 --> 00:24:27.756
and give very generous financial aid.

00:24:27.756 --> 00:24:31.816
So don't let your family's
finances narrow the aperture

00:24:31.816 --> 00:24:35.436
of where you might wanna go to college.

00:24:35.436 --> 00:24:38.493
Let's see, from Facebook, Maya Campbell,

00:24:38.493 --> 00:24:40.106
"If you weren't leading Khan Academy,

00:24:40.106 --> 00:24:42.743
what do you think you'd be doing instead?"

00:24:44.716 --> 00:24:45.916
So there's a bunch of projects

00:24:45.916 --> 00:24:48.256
that I think in the
education space generally

00:24:48.256 --> 00:24:49.216
I've always been interested

00:24:49.216 --> 00:24:51.316
in the stuff we're doing at Khan Academy.

00:24:51.316 --> 00:24:52.406
Some of y'all know I've been working

00:24:52.406 --> 00:24:54.956
on this project at schoolhouse.world,

00:24:54.956 --> 00:24:56.646
very complimentary to Khan Academy

00:24:56.646 --> 00:24:57.846
around peer to peer tutoring.

00:24:57.846 --> 00:24:58.876
Although I view that as all part

00:24:58.876 --> 00:25:01.136
of the same broader mission,

00:25:01.136 --> 00:25:03.826
ways for students to know what they know

00:25:03.826 --> 00:25:05.496
so that they can approve it

00:25:07.516 --> 00:25:09.906
for things like a college admissions.

00:25:09.906 --> 00:25:11.396
Uni of Chicago just announced a couple

00:25:11.396 --> 00:25:12.229
of weeks ago that they were gonna

00:25:12.229 --> 00:25:15.356
use these schoolhouse.world certifications

00:25:15.356 --> 00:25:16.976
for admission cycle this year.

00:25:16.976 --> 00:25:20.126
So I think I'd be drawn.

00:25:20.126 --> 00:25:21.266
Even when I was working at the hedge fund,

00:25:21.266 --> 00:25:22.586
I was saying I'll start a school one day

00:25:22.586 --> 00:25:24.196
if I'm able to make enough
money in the hedge fund

00:25:24.196 --> 00:25:25.046
'cause I always thought it'd be fun

00:25:25.046 --> 00:25:26.806
to be a Dumbledore-type figure.

00:25:26.806 --> 00:25:28.306
So I think I would be doing something

00:25:28.306 --> 00:25:30.876
in education eventually.

00:25:30.876 --> 00:25:33.926
If you're saying if I wasn't
working in education at all,

00:25:33.926 --> 00:25:35.646
what would I be doing?

00:25:35.646 --> 00:25:38.306
Maybe I would've stayed
in the hedge fund world

00:25:38.306 --> 00:25:41.196
to some degree and tried to become...

00:25:41.196 --> 00:25:44.536
But even then I would have
viewed that as a means to an end.

00:25:44.536 --> 00:25:48.216
And you know, I think my biggest interests

00:25:48.216 --> 00:25:51.356
are I mean, my general
interest is in human potential.

00:25:51.356 --> 00:25:53.296
And so that's why I'm so
interested in education.

00:25:53.296 --> 00:25:54.946
And then a more recent passion

00:25:54.946 --> 00:25:56.126
that I've really been developing,

00:25:56.126 --> 00:25:58.046
y'all have heard me talk
about it on this live stream

00:25:58.046 --> 00:26:00.386
is just kind of going
on an inward journey,

00:26:00.386 --> 00:26:03.066
like understanding who we
are as sentient beings.

00:26:03.066 --> 00:26:05.646
And so that's why I've
been meditating a lot.

00:26:05.646 --> 00:26:08.076
I've been actually investing time

00:26:08.076 --> 00:26:09.456
to get to know my friends better.

00:26:09.456 --> 00:26:12.866
We meet on Sundays and we have
these, now we do it on Zoom,

00:26:12.866 --> 00:26:14.116
where we have these conversations

00:26:14.116 --> 00:26:15.986
about what does it mean
to be a good person

00:26:15.986 --> 00:26:19.516
and what's perception versus
reality and things like that.

00:26:19.516 --> 00:26:21.756
So maybe I would have become some type

00:26:21.756 --> 00:26:26.756
of community builder type of
person who knows, who knows.

00:26:30.126 --> 00:26:32.756
I also like, on the side

00:26:32.756 --> 00:26:36.216
I think I'm always drawn
to kind of real estate.

00:26:36.216 --> 00:26:37.246
Maybe I would have been some type

00:26:37.246 --> 00:26:39.196
of like home fixer upper.

00:26:39.196 --> 00:26:41.783
There's something very
tangible about that.

00:26:41.783 --> 00:26:43.833
Like, that's just a random other interest

00:26:45.226 --> 00:26:46.976
that I might've had.

00:26:46.976 --> 00:26:48.476
A lot of my friends know...

00:26:48.476 --> 00:26:51.186
Anyone who I know who
wants to buy a house,

00:26:51.186 --> 00:26:52.576
they turn to me right now.

00:26:52.576 --> 00:26:55.226
I'm like their shadow real estate broker

00:26:55.226 --> 00:26:57.166
'cause it's just kind of a fun thing

00:26:57.166 --> 00:27:00.519
that I like to do when I'm
procrastinating. (laughs)

00:27:01.735 --> 00:27:05.068
So, Fataha Babur is asking from YouTube,

00:27:06.073 --> 00:27:07.853
"How do I learn how to teach?"

00:27:09.246 --> 00:27:11.566
Well, the best way to learn
anything is to keep doing it,

00:27:11.566 --> 00:27:14.926
getting feedback and really
processing that feedback.

00:27:14.926 --> 00:27:18.206
My general, you know, if I were to give...

00:27:18.206 --> 00:27:19.756
I haven't thought a lot
about this, but you know,

00:27:19.756 --> 00:27:22.446
step one, learn the material
really, really well yourself.

00:27:22.446 --> 00:27:24.306
Not only so that it's
really intuitive for you,

00:27:24.306 --> 00:27:25.746
but you're excited about it.

00:27:25.746 --> 00:27:26.876
'Cause if you don't have excitement

00:27:26.876 --> 00:27:27.836
and if it's not intuitive to you,

00:27:27.836 --> 00:27:29.676
it's gonna be very hard
to convey the excitement

00:27:29.676 --> 00:27:30.726
and the intuition to someone else.

00:27:30.726 --> 00:27:31.846
So that's step one.

00:27:31.846 --> 00:27:34.516
The second thing is try
to build the empathy

00:27:34.516 --> 00:27:36.966
of really putting yourself
in the mind of the person

00:27:36.966 --> 00:27:38.606
you're trying to explain to.

00:27:38.606 --> 00:27:41.046
Don't try to force the
knowledge into them,

00:27:41.046 --> 00:27:46.006
cater to their needs, be
as empathetic as possible,

00:27:46.006 --> 00:27:47.636
be as patient as possible,

00:27:47.636 --> 00:27:51.536
try to meet them where they
are and just be conversational.

00:27:51.536 --> 00:27:53.776
Don't talk down, don't talk above them.

00:27:53.776 --> 00:27:55.486
Just say look, we're just two souls

00:27:55.486 --> 00:27:57.156
trying to understand this.

00:27:57.156 --> 00:27:58.876
I just happen to know it already

00:27:58.876 --> 00:28:00.466
and let's see how I can help you.

00:28:00.466 --> 00:28:02.536
And I think if you have
that kind of relaxed

00:28:02.536 --> 00:28:06.246
but still passionate and very
intuitive angle on something,

00:28:06.246 --> 00:28:09.356
people are gonna really
enjoy learning with you

00:28:09.356 --> 00:28:10.886
and going on a learning adventure.

00:28:10.886 --> 00:28:13.646
That's my gut sense.

00:28:13.646 --> 00:28:15.226
Time's going by fast.

00:28:15.226 --> 00:28:17.069
Let me do a couple more questions.

00:28:18.243 --> 00:28:20.386
"What skill do you think is the
most useful for the future?"

00:28:20.386 --> 00:28:23.429
This from Khurram Amir
Mazar from Facebook.

00:28:27.537 --> 00:28:30.656
The meta-skill is the
ability to learn things.

00:28:30.656 --> 00:28:32.386
The one thing we know
about the next century

00:28:32.386 --> 00:28:35.636
is that the rate of change
is only going to accelerate.

00:28:35.636 --> 00:28:36.786
There's these famous stats,

00:28:36.786 --> 00:28:37.836
I don't know how they come up with it,

00:28:37.836 --> 00:28:39.156
that the majority of the jobs

00:28:39.156 --> 00:28:40.906
that all of y'all are going to have

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:44.216
10, 20, 30 years from now
don't exist yet today.

00:28:44.216 --> 00:28:45.866
So we don't even know what those jobs are.

00:28:45.866 --> 00:28:47.336
And so the real skill you need to learn

00:28:47.336 --> 00:28:49.526
is how do you pull knowledge.

00:28:49.526 --> 00:28:50.756
And the beauty is right now

00:28:50.756 --> 00:28:52.596
in the internet through Khan
Academy and other things,

00:28:52.596 --> 00:28:53.626
the knowledge is all there.

00:28:53.626 --> 00:28:56.556
There's a million ways
to learn the skills.

00:28:56.556 --> 00:28:58.166
And so you just have to
get good at doing it.

00:28:58.166 --> 00:28:59.049
So when you're on Khan Academy,

00:28:59.049 --> 00:29:00.973
yes, you can learn to factor a polynomial

00:29:00.973 --> 00:29:02.976
and you can learn about photosynthesis

00:29:02.976 --> 00:29:06.096
and you can learn about
the American Revolution

00:29:06.096 --> 00:29:08.186
and that's all important content to learn

00:29:08.186 --> 00:29:09.406
and skills to learn,

00:29:09.406 --> 00:29:12.036
but that meta-skill of
learning at your own time

00:29:12.036 --> 00:29:15.726
and space and using tools
at your disposal to do so,

00:29:15.726 --> 00:29:18.486
that's really an incredible skill.

00:29:18.486 --> 00:29:19.356
And based on the last question,

00:29:19.356 --> 00:29:21.476
I think the ability to teach others

00:29:21.476 --> 00:29:23.926
is an incredibly valuable skill,

00:29:23.926 --> 00:29:25.846
not just for becoming a teacher.

00:29:25.846 --> 00:29:27.876
If you think about what a good manager

00:29:27.876 --> 00:29:29.506
does in a corporation,

00:29:29.506 --> 00:29:31.226
they're good supporters and teachers

00:29:31.226 --> 00:29:32.639
and mentors of other people.

00:29:34.746 --> 00:29:36.176
You need to communicate when you teach,

00:29:36.176 --> 00:29:37.526
you need to empathize when you teach,

00:29:37.526 --> 00:29:39.036
you need to inspire when you teach.

00:29:39.036 --> 00:29:39.936
And if you think about it,

00:29:39.936 --> 00:29:41.926
those three things are
completely synonymous

00:29:41.926 --> 00:29:45.186
with leadership or any type
of entrepreneurship as well.

00:29:45.186 --> 00:29:47.376
So I would say the ability to teach

00:29:47.376 --> 00:29:49.846
and the ability to learn new things.

00:29:49.846 --> 00:29:53.256
Let's see, one more question,

00:29:53.256 --> 00:29:55.433
or maybe I'll do two
real fast, YouTube, MMBL,

00:29:55.433 --> 00:29:57.846
"They say Khan Academy is
a great area for SAT prep.

00:29:57.846 --> 00:30:00.716
I really need to raise my
score 200 points or more.

00:30:00.716 --> 00:30:02.466
What do you advise me to do?"

00:30:02.466 --> 00:30:05.156
You know, if you go on Khan Academy,

00:30:05.156 --> 00:30:07.256
use the official SAT
practice which we have

00:30:07.256 --> 00:30:10.686
in partnership with the
college board and you put in,

00:30:10.686 --> 00:30:12.466
I don't know when you're
planning on taking the SAT,

00:30:12.466 --> 00:30:14.706
I'm guessing you might
be a sophomore or junior.

00:30:14.706 --> 00:30:16.136
It's a little late now if you're applying

00:30:16.136 --> 00:30:19.939
for college right now but
maybe you are, don't stress.

00:30:20.946 --> 00:30:24.516
But you know, I would put
in 20/30 minutes a day,

00:30:24.516 --> 00:30:26.186
maybe four or five days a week.

00:30:26.186 --> 00:30:29.026
And you know, we actually
have done research.

00:30:29.026 --> 00:30:30.996
If you follow the recommendations

00:30:30.996 --> 00:30:32.276
on the official SAT practice,

00:30:32.276 --> 00:30:34.566
the stuff that the software's
saying you need to work on,

00:30:34.566 --> 00:30:37.696
you really focus on
leveling up your skills

00:30:37.696 --> 00:30:39.829
to kind of higher and higher levels on it.

00:30:41.326 --> 00:30:43.956
And every now and then you
take full-length practice tests

00:30:43.956 --> 00:30:46.446
in as realistic format as possible,

00:30:46.446 --> 00:30:48.086
we see in the data that that's really

00:30:48.086 --> 00:30:49.556
gonna drive a lot of growth.

00:30:49.556 --> 00:30:52.366
And especially if you have
some time before your SAT

00:30:52.366 --> 00:30:55.156
and you just make a habit
20/30 minutes a day,

00:30:55.156 --> 00:30:56.236
follow the recommendations,

00:30:56.236 --> 00:30:57.726
work on what you need to work on,

00:30:57.726 --> 00:30:59.626
take those full-length practice tests.

00:30:59.626 --> 00:31:00.806
I think in a few months

00:31:00.806 --> 00:31:03.616
you're going to see
pretty noticeable gains.

00:31:03.616 --> 00:31:04.996
And if you have even
more than a few months,

00:31:04.996 --> 00:31:06.606
I actually feel pretty confident

00:31:06.606 --> 00:31:09.486
you'll be able to reach your goals

00:31:09.486 --> 00:31:11.336
and maybe it'll happen sooner.

00:31:11.336 --> 00:31:14.419
And then, we're all over time.

00:31:16.586 --> 00:31:19.093
This one, YouTube, Aten Mera says,

00:31:19.093 --> 00:31:21.846
"How do you have so much depth
of knowledge in most domains?

00:31:21.846 --> 00:31:23.156
It seems to be quite extraordinary

00:31:23.156 --> 00:31:24.126
for a single person to do."

00:31:24.126 --> 00:31:29.126
well, I appreciate that
you feel that I have

00:31:30.576 --> 00:31:33.026
this depth and, you know, look,

00:31:33.026 --> 00:31:35.376
that is one of the things
that why I feel lucky

00:31:35.376 --> 00:31:38.386
to have this job that I
get to exercise that depth

00:31:38.386 --> 00:31:41.706
in many domains and then
communicate it to other folks.

00:31:41.706 --> 00:31:43.866
But I think we're all just curious

00:31:43.866 --> 00:31:45.856
beings in this thing we call life,

00:31:45.856 --> 00:31:47.466
in this universe that
we're trying to understand.

00:31:47.466 --> 00:31:50.556
And almost all knowledge is
just trying to make sense

00:31:50.556 --> 00:31:53.626
of who we are and what
the universe is all about.

00:31:53.626 --> 00:31:56.606
And what I've found is if
you can draw connections

00:31:56.606 --> 00:31:59.246
in one domain, it translates
into other domains

00:31:59.246 --> 00:32:00.546
more than you might suspect.

00:32:00.546 --> 00:32:01.816
I think in a traditional school system

00:32:01.816 --> 00:32:04.556
we silo the subject so
much that you don't realize

00:32:04.556 --> 00:32:08.846
that ways of learning and ways
of connection in your brain

00:32:08.846 --> 00:32:10.136
are not as siloed as you think.

00:32:10.136 --> 00:32:12.106
It's not like your brain
has like a math section

00:32:12.106 --> 00:32:13.666
and then separately a physics section

00:32:13.666 --> 00:32:16.736
and then separately an art section.

00:32:16.736 --> 00:32:18.856
These neurons are all jumbled together.

00:32:18.856 --> 00:32:22.606
And so for me, it's very
fun to ponder things,

00:32:22.606 --> 00:32:25.376
to learn things, to be
able to communicate things.

00:32:25.376 --> 00:32:27.266
And I think if you just have
the mindset for yourself,

00:32:27.266 --> 00:32:28.836
like I'm just gonna keep learning,

00:32:28.836 --> 00:32:29.996
and I'm just a curious person

00:32:29.996 --> 00:32:32.576
and I too wanna learn everything.

00:32:32.576 --> 00:32:35.156
I think when you're
sitting in your mid-forties

00:32:35.156 --> 00:32:38.799
on a live stream and your
beard is graying a little bit,

00:32:39.796 --> 00:32:43.086
people will be sending you
questions like you just sent me.

00:32:43.086 --> 00:32:44.606
So thanks everyone for this.

00:32:44.606 --> 00:32:47.866
It's always super fun these
these ask me anythings.

00:32:47.866 --> 00:32:50.536
A couple of announcements,

00:32:50.536 --> 00:32:53.446
tomorrow, October 22nd.

00:32:53.446 --> 00:32:55.656
I don't what day is today
is today. Is today the?

00:32:55.656 --> 00:32:57.256
The day after tomorrow

00:32:57.256 --> 00:32:59.936
we're going to have a motivation webinar

00:32:59.936 --> 00:33:03.356
from Kristen DiCerbo, our
chief learning officer.

00:33:03.356 --> 00:33:04.996
And I highly recommend this.

00:33:04.996 --> 00:33:06.776
Kristen is great.

00:33:06.776 --> 00:33:09.366
She is an expert on all things learning

00:33:09.366 --> 00:33:11.446
and she is also very engaging herself.

00:33:11.446 --> 00:33:16.436
So encourage parents to
check out this webinar

00:33:16.436 --> 00:33:18.556
on how to motivate middle
and high school kids

00:33:18.556 --> 00:33:20.686
during distance learning.

00:33:20.686 --> 00:33:23.316
Also wanna remind folks that tomorrow

00:33:23.316 --> 00:33:25.366
we're going to have Pedro Noguera,

00:33:25.366 --> 00:33:27.706
the dean of the University
of Southern California's

00:33:27.706 --> 00:33:29.656
Rossier School of Education.

00:33:29.656 --> 00:33:32.756
And so start thinking of
questions for Dean Noguera.

00:33:32.756 --> 00:33:34.866
That's going to be a great conversation.

00:33:34.866 --> 00:33:36.696
So, everyone thanks for joining.

00:33:36.696 --> 00:33:38.916
These ask me any things are always fun

00:33:38.916 --> 00:33:41.416
and I will see you tomorrow.

00:33:41.416 --> 00:33:42.249
Onward.

